  "slug": "microbiome-metagenomics-9f55",
  "summary": "Host identity structures marine holobiont communities, while metaEukaryome shows eukaryotic reads hidden in global metagenomes.",
  "synthesis": "Current items link microbial community structure to host identity and to eukaryotes recovered as bycatch in bacterial-focused sequencing. A 16S amplicon survey of the Antarctic stalked jellyfish Haliclystus antarcticus compares bacterial communities across gut, peduncle and tentacles, and reports antimicrobial activity from a gut-associated Oceanisphaera ostreae isolate. A three-year 16S survey of four South Florida octocoral species finds that host identity drives stable microbiomes dominated by host-specific Endozoicomonas. Across these marker-gene studies, host identity is the organizing variable. In contrast, metaEukaryome uses mitochondrial and plastid sequences to detect eukaryotes in 75,727 global metagenomes, revealing cross-domain co-occurrence. The new element is that eukaryotic sequences present in bacterial metagenome datasets can be systematically recovered and analysed.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
